# The Scheme notation lips emits into: what grounds a name in a clause, as data.
# The kernel reads this file and enumerates nothing itself.
#
# A clause is one named definition, and this is the word that makes one. The
# kernel knows the shape, never the word.
definer define

# A binder brings names into scope for its body; the index says where its names
# sit, because (lambda (x) ...) and (let ((x 1)) ...) carry them differently.
binder lambda params 1
binder let bindings 1
binder let* bindings 1
binder letrec bindings 1

# Special forms that bind nothing.
form cond
form case
form else
form if
form when
form unless
form and
form or
form not
form quote

# The base notation: present on every runtime, carrying no authority. A clause
# needs no more than this to be a pure first-order definition; anything that
# touches the world is a contract instead.
procedure cons car cdr caar cadr cdar cddr
procedure null? pair? list list? length append reverse
procedure eq? eqv? equal?
procedure + - * / = < > <= >= min max abs
procedure number? string? boolean? symbol? char? procedure?
procedure string=? string<? string-length string-append substring string->number number->string
procedure char=?
procedure apply
